# See everything an agent did

```elixir
Mix.install([{:wardrik, "~> 0.1.0-rc.2"}, {:kino, "~> 0.19"}])
```

## What you'll build

By the end, you watch an agent's events arrive as it works, read its run back step by step, see
who changed what, and have your own module react when a run ends.

## Setup

Give the agent one tool, so its run has a step to show:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Notes do
  @behaviour WardrikCore.Capability

  @impl true
  def tools(_context, _opts) do
    [
      WardrikCore.Tool.new!(
        name: "list_notes",
        description: "Lists Maria's notes.",
        run: fn _args, _context -> {:ok, ["Call Initech about the Q3 invoice.", "Book Berlin trip."]} end,
        annotations: [read_only: true]
      )
    ]
  end
end

{:ok, _} = Wardrik.start_link(name: MyApp.AgentRuntime, capabilities: [notes: MyApp.Notes])

scope = Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "acme/@maria", roles: [:owner], resources: ["acme/@maria/*"])

{:ok, agent} =
  Wardrik.Agent.define(scope, "acme/@maria/assistant",
    model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5",
    instructions: "You keep Maria's notes. Be brief.",
    tools: ["notes"],
    policy: [budget: [usd: 0.25, per: :day]]
  )
```

## React to events in your own module

A listener hands your module each event it follows, with the cursor past it. This one posts a line
to a frame each time a run ends:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.RunAlerts do
  use Wardrik.Listener,
    wardrik: MyApp.AgentRuntime,
    types: ["wardrik.run.completed", "wardrik.run.failed"]

  @impl true
  def handle(%Wardrik.Event{} = event, listener) do
    line = "**#{event.type}** for `#{event.subject}` at #{Calendar.strftime(event.time, "%H:%M:%S")}"
    Kino.Frame.append(listener.options[:frame], Kino.Markdown.new(line))
    :ok
  end
end

alerts = Kino.Frame.new() |> Kino.render()
{:ok, _} = Kino.start_child({MyApp.RunAlerts, frame: alerts})

Kino.nothing()
```

The frame stays empty until a run ends. Options you start it with, such as `frame:`, reach
`handle/2` in `listener.options`.

In your app, save `listener.cursor` with your work and return it from `cursor/1`. The listener
then carries on from it after a restart. See
[Events and the audit](https://hexdocs.pm/wardrik/events.html).

## Watch events as they're written

Take the cursor where the log stands. Then follow every event after it into a table:

```elixir
{:ok, cursor} = Wardrik.Events.head(scope)

live = Kino.Frame.new() |> Kino.render()

row = fn event ->
  %{
    time: Calendar.strftime(event.time, "%H:%M:%S.%f"),
    type: event.type,
    subject: event.subject
  }
end

Kino.start_child(
  {Task,
   fn ->
     {:ok, events} = Wardrik.Events.stream_list(scope, after: cursor, resources: ["acme/@maria/*"])

     events
     |> Stream.reject(fn {event, _cursor} -> String.ends_with?(event.type, ".delta") end)
     |> Enum.reduce([], fn {event, _cursor}, rows ->
       rows = rows ++ [row.(event)]
       Kino.Frame.render(live, Kino.DataTable.new(rows, keys: [:time, :type, :subject]))
       rows
     end)
   end}
)

Kino.nothing()
```

The stream never ends. It leaves out `message.delta` events, one per chunk of text, to keep the
table short.

Now ask the agent something. This calls a model, so add your Anthropic key as `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`
in the Secrets panel (the key icon on the left). It's two small calls, under a cent.

```elixir
System.put_env("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY", System.fetch_env!("LB_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"))
```

Watch the table above fill:

```elixir
{:ok, reply} = Wardrik.Agent.reply(scope, agent, "What's on my notes?")
reply.text
```

The run starts, calls the model, calls `list_notes`, calls the model again, and completes. The
listener's frame shows `wardrik.run.completed`.

## Read a run back

The timeline is the conversation as a person reads it. Each run lists its steps:

```elixir
{:ok, timeline} = Wardrik.Agent.timeline(scope, agent)

steps =
  for %{"kind" => "run"} = run <- timeline.items, step <- run["steps"] do
    %{
      run: String.slice(run["id"], 0, 12),
      step: step["type"],
      detail: step["name"] || step["text"],
      status: step["status"]
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(steps, keys: [:run, :step, :detail, :status], name: "Steps")
```

`Wardrik.Agent.runs/3` sums each run up:

```elixir
{:ok, runs} = Wardrik.Agent.runs(scope, agent)

Kino.DataTable.new(
  Enum.map(runs.runs, &Map.from_struct/1),
  keys: [:status, :model, :duration_ms, :tool_calls, :input_tokens, :output_tokens, :cost_micro_usd],
  name: "Runs"
)
```

## Read the log back

Every event is kept. Read them back a page at a time, after a cursor or from the start:

```elixir
{:ok, page} = Wardrik.Agent.events(scope, agent, limit: 100)

page.events
|> Enum.frequencies_by(& &1.type)
|> Enum.map(fn {type, count} -> %{type: type, count: count} end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: [:type, :count], name: "Events by type")
```

Each event is a [CloudEvent](https://cloudevents.io). Its `data` has the shape the protocol gives
its type. Pass `page.next` as `after:` to read the next page.

## Who changed what

Agents' events say what agents did. The audit says what people did: defining, deciding, granting.
Change Maria's agent, then read the audit. It needs a grant on `deployment`:

```elixir
{:ok, _agent} =
  Wardrik.Agent.define(scope, "acme/@maria/assistant",
    model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5",
    instructions: "You keep Maria's notes. Answer in bullet points.",
    tools: ["notes"],
    policy: [budget: [usd: 0.25, per: :day]]
  )

operator = Wardrik.grant(scope, roles: [:owner], resources: ["deployment"])

{:ok, audit} = Wardrik.Audit.list(operator, limit: 50)

audit.events
|> Enum.map(fn event ->
  %{
    time: Calendar.strftime(event.time, "%H:%M:%S"),
    type: event.type,
    actor: event.data["actor"]["id"],
    subject: event.subject
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: [:time, :type, :actor, :subject], name: "Audit")
```

